/bin/bash -lc "cat > /home/codex/.cache/ladon-benefit-r62-w4ypswr2/reader-output/exposition-ordinary/revisions.json <<'EOF'
{
  \"revisions\": [
    {
      \"id\": \"passage-17\",
      \"original_text\": \"The supplied uniform transcript targets formally establish both transcript and average-only optimality under the sufficient condition.\",
      \"revised_text\": \"The supplied uniform targets formally establish transcript optimality under the sufficient condition; the average-only optimality assertion is supported by the exposition but is conventional and has no supplied formal target.\",
      \"reason\": \"The exposition's all-horizons corollary asserts both transcript and average-only comparisons under the sufficient condition. However, the supplied formal targets and assessment cover transcript privacy and total variance (including the full-batch reference), while the average-only component is explicitly assessed as conventional-only. This wording distinguishes formal support from the unassessed/formalization gap without treating that gap as evidence against the mathematical claim.\"
    },
    {
      \"id\": \"passage-42\",
      \"original_text\": \"Let point be a fixed-epoch point with point.epoch < point.horizon, h > 0, boundary ≥ 0 and query > boundary. If fixedEpochCenterGap point h boundary ≥ 0, the propagation lemma gives fixedEpochCenterGap point h query > 0.\",
      \"revised_text\": \"Let point be a fixed-epoch point with point.epoch < point.horizon, h > 0, boundary ≥ 0 and query > boundary. If fixedEpochCenterGap point h boundary ≥ 0, the propagation lemma gives fixedEpochCenterGap point h query > 0.\",
      \"reason\": \"No revision is warranted. The formal theorem fixedEpochCenterGap_pos_of_boundary_nonneg has precisely these hypotheses and conclusion. Its source proof uses a tilted strictly convex function; the exposition presents an equivalent Gaussian-kernel ratio argument, so the formal result supports the implication but does not certify that proof strategy or prose correspondence independently.\"
    },
    {
      \"id\": \"passage-68\",
      \"original_text\": \"Let point be a fixed-epoch point with point.epoch < point.horizon, h > 0, boundary ≥ 0 and query > boundary. The propagation lemma gives fixedEpochCenterGap point h query > 0.\",
      \"revised_text\": \"Let point be a fixed-epoch point with point.epoch < point.horizon, h > 0, boundary ≥ 0 and query > boundary, and suppose fixedEpochCenterGap point h boundary ≥ 0. Then the propagation lemma gives fixedEpochCenterGap point h query > 0.\",
      \"reason\": \"The original omitted the boundary-gap premise required by the formal theorem and by the exposition's lemma. Adding it repairs the implication. This narrows the original unconditional assertion to the theorem's actual conditional statement; the formal result does not establish the stronger assertion without that premise.\"
    }
  ]
}
EOF
lean --version && lean TaskOne.lean"

